alsa: take the time64 timespec fix; cpal from master, rodio from a fork

On Debian trixie, 32-bit libasound writes a 16-byte `struct timespec`.
`libc::timespec` stays 8 bytes on arm-unknown-linux-gnueabihf, so
`Status::get_htstamp` writes 8 bytes past the slot its caller reserved,
overwrites the saved lr and returns into address 0. cpal asks for that
timestamp on every callback, so it takes the first note played.

The fix spans three crates:

  alsa-sys 0.6.1     diwic/alsa-sys#20, released 2026-07-31 — compiles a
                     probe measuring sizeof(snd_htimestamp_t) against the
                     ALSA headers and, when it is 16, defines alsa-sys' own
                     16-byte timespec instead of re-exporting libc's.
  alsa 0.12.1        diwic/alsa-rs#158, released 2026-07-31 — `alsa::timespec`
                     (alsa-sys') replaces libc::timespec in pcm, rawmidi
                     and ump.
  cpal master        RustAudio/cpal#1285, squash-merged as f040088 — bumps
                     alsa to 0.12.1 and alsa-sys to 0.6.1 and carries
                     timestamps as `alsa::timespec`. No 0.18.x release will
                     carry it: 0.18.2 (2026-08-16) still asks for alsa ^0.11,
                     and master is already versioned 0.19.0.

So the two alsa crates are plain version requirements (the patch releases —
alsa 0.12.0 still takes its timespec from libc, alsa-sys 0.6.0 still
re-exports it), and cpal is pinned to a master rev.

An earlier revision of this patch pinned the head of the then-open #1285.
That rev is now unreachable from every ref in the cpal repo — the PR was
force-pushed and then squash-merged, so none of its branch commits landed on
master — which left the pin one GitHub gc away from breaking every arch at
once. A master rev cannot disappear.

rodio is the reason a fork enters the picture. qbz never names cpal: it asks
for `rodio = "0.22"` and takes whatever cpal rodio pulls in. Released rodio
0.22.2 requires cpal ^0.17 — that is why upstream's lock holds 0.17.3 — and
rodio master requires ^0.18. Neither matches a 0.19.0 cpal, and cargo's
fallback for an unmatched [patch] would resolve rodio's cpal from crates.io,
a second cpal/alsa-sys pair, which `links = "alsa"` forbids. Nor is there a
cpal rev that would satisfy rodio master as it stands: master was renumbered
0.19.0 by 5a53a0e (2026-05-27), two months BEFORE the alsa fix f040088
(2026-07-31), so every rev carrying the fix reads 0.19.0. So rodio comes
from b0bbywan/rodio branch `cpal-0.19`: rodio master (880aaba) plus the
one-line `cpal = "0.19"` bump upstream will make itself at that release. It
still declares version 0.22.2, so qbz's `rodio = "0.22"` requirement is
unchanged.

Across the 0.18 → 0.19 bump the only cpal API change rodio trips is a
rename (`StreamTrait::play` deprecated in favour of `start` — two warnings,
old name still works). rodio master raises MSRV to rustc 1.95, which the
builders' rustup-installed stable satisfies. Revs rather than branches, so a
rebuild resolves the same sources.

qbz DOES name a cpal type now, which is why this patch stopped being
manifest-only at v2.1.1. The #660 fix added qbz-audio/src/stream_health.rs,
written against cpal 0.17's `StreamError` enum — upstream's lock resolves
0.17.3, so it compiles there. cpal folded every error into one
`Error` + `ErrorKind` pair in 0.18, so `rodio::cpal::StreamError` does not
exist behind any cpal this patch can pin, released or not. The triage now
reads `err.kind()`:

  DeviceNotAvailable      Wedged, as before.
  StreamInvalidated       Wedged. New: cpal says outright that the stream
                          has to be rebuilt, which is exactly the verdict.
  BackendError + POLLERR  Wedged. The 0.17 text match, kept as a guard.
  everything else         Transient, as before.

The irony is that the pinned cpal fixes the bug stream_health.rs exists to
survive: its ALSA worker calls prepare() on an xrun, try_resume() on a
suspend, and reports a disconnect as DeviceNotAvailable, so the POLLERR
spin cannot happen and no xrun ever reaches the callback. Hence the two
test cases that constructed `StreamError::BufferUnderrun` — a variant with
no 0.19 counterpart, because an underrun is no longer an event a stream
hears about — now use `DeviceChanged`, the reroute that likewise leaves the
stream running. The four call sites pass type-inferred closures and are
untouched.

Still needed on v2.1.1 (2026-09-11): crates.io has cpal 0.18.2 and rodio
0.22.2, cpal 0.18.2 still asks for alsa ^0.11 / alsa-sys ^0.4 so no release
carries the fix, upstream asks for alsa 0.11, and its lock resolves cpal
0.17.3. The workspace's own [patch.crates-io] holds two entries as of this
tag (a vendored rust_cast and a path rupnp), so the two pins join it instead
of creating the section.

Verified with `cargo tree --locked -p qbzd -e normal`: a single alsa-sys
0.6.1 (crates.io) in the graph, alsa 0.12.1 (crates.io), cpal 0.19.0 (git
master), rodio 0.22.2 (git fork); `cargo check --locked -p qbzd` and
`cargo test --locked -p qbz-audio --lib stream_health` (7 tests) both pass
on amd64. cpal's PulseAudio backend stays in the graph via rodio's
default features; the `pulseaudio` crate is pure Rust (no build script, no
libpulse), so the NEEDED list is unchanged.

Drop this patch once cpal 0.19 is released and rodio requires it; the fork
branch, the alsa version requirements and the stream_health.rs hunk die with
it — that last one reverts to upstream's file only if upstream has moved to
the unified cpal error API by then, which it must have to build at all.

